model {
  extend basalt.modelGateway {
    metadata {
      constraint "A refused runtime handoff command keeps why it was refused. The controller logs one closed line per refusal with the command action, candidate commit, operation where the command has one, failure step and reason, and returns the same fixed step, reason and code. The release barrier reads at most a small refusal body, keeps only values from that fixed vocabulary in its refusal delta, and records anything else as unclassified; refusal text and other body fields never reach evidence."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "The ordinary image release proves its account credential through the deployed controller Secrets Store binding before adoption, idle application creation or fleet configuration. This read-only observation cannot start compute or change controller permission. The controller code phase must activate the binding first and can already update runtime adapters, admitted storage and release journals. A missing binding, provider refusal, identity drift or stale observation stops progression. Exact provider identities remain in an authenticated encrypted receipt; the public receipt retains hashes, closed status and safe refusal deltas. The controller exports only allowlisted provider failure codes; the operator preserves those codes without reading exception text or provider response bodies. Abort inspection without a retained submission reports not-sent and cannot write or send a stop."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "The account-owned observation credential is provisioned separately and stored directly in the deployment account's Secrets Store. Deployment binds only the stable secret name and never creates an API token, reads its value or depends on a person's connection. Provisioning requires positive account token-write authority and an explicit writer decision; an unknown create reply never authorizes another credential. Receipts retain only token identity and secret name. Application provisioning admits only the exact source declaration and cannot apply unrelated source or resources."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Operator fleet writes require an expiring writer GO whose digest covers the exact command, complete fleet configuration, controller code, destination and bridge key digest. The signed transport receives a fresh envelope of at most sixty seconds at dispatch, while the reviewed action and payload remain fixed. The private bridge sends once and closes on every outcome. The operator retains a durable submission marker before sending; repeated execution can only inspect that same operation, even after its writer GO expires. A lost acknowledgment is recovered by inspecting the same retained operation, including after the writer lease expires, without reissuing a mutation. Fleet economics depend on removing each Home node's idle database work across warming, standby and draining states; more ready nodes alone are not evidence of lower total cost."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Ordinary production releases retain blue and green immutable same-account image pins from native state. The PoolWorker alchemy deploy is the only writer of the blue and green classes and their migrations, through the reviewed add-sqlite-class intent. Provisioning never uploads the Worker: a missing application is provisioned idle on the existing SQLite class, before that class has a container, and the next code phase binds it. Explicit immutable pins remain available for independent provisioning. Blue and green have independent container namespaces, zero initial instances and provider maxima of four, while the single controller ledger limits all live and uncertain extra resources to four across both applications. The account-owned observation token has only container inventory read permission for that deployment account. A separately provisioned account Secrets Store secret is bound by stable name; deployment never creates an API token or reads its value. Missing or unreadable secrets produce unknown observation, prevent launch and retirement intent, and never prove absence. Each observation rereads the binding so provisioning restores observation without a Worker restart. Native global fetch is invoked without the reader as its receiver. Provider reads use workerd-compatible manual redirects and require status 200, so neither credentials nor observations follow a redirect. Provider transport exceptions retain only a closed provider_fetch_failed cause through signed operator refusal; exception messages, credentials and provider bodies never enter that response. An enabled fleet may resume a reserved launch only when its durable first-start intent is absent, proving compute was never dispatched; a retained intent permits observation only, and disabled permission cannot start compute. The root rechecks current fleet permission in the same storage transaction that commits first-start intent, fencing provider waits against an acknowledged disable. Only the production deploy jobs holding the shared Actions writer lease may construct ordinary release authority. Each admitted application creation retains its exact manifest before a one-shot provider send. An image update freezes the inactive application durably and positively proves it empty before its provider mutation. Active, candidate, draining, warming and uncertain resources prevent that application from being changed. Missing pins cannot delete previously declared infrastructure through the guarded release path. No image pin or declared binding grants permission to start compute."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Provisioning attaches an application only to an existing SQLite class that has no container yet, and accepts only the original singleton or the exact blue and green classes. Each fleet application starts with zero instances and a provider maximum of four; class, binding, resource and journal identities must agree. Each application requires its own retained manifest and an expiring explicit operator GO or the exact ordinary release candidate and production writer lease. The manifest retains the exact active namespace and boot. Plaintext startup settings stay inside encrypted state, separate from public receipts. Unknown application creation remains observation-only. Provisioning alone neither enables fleet growth nor expands the shared four-resource ledger guard. Stage root and endpoint observations read their own production records independently across DO stores; their coordinator joins the snapshots. These reads cannot probe, start, drain or change the retained lifecycle."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "The existing primary uses its recorded immutable registry reference and original runtime input hash while controller code and idle applications are prepared. The ordinary release builds only the inactive application. Actual provider version, image digest, startup witness, health and authenticated public models serving precede atomic promotion. Promotion switches all new routing and Home destinations together while held bodies retain the old compute. The inactive-image freeze and primary headroom reservation survive reconstruction, respect the existing configured fleet maximum and retain uncertain capacity until exact absence. A selected non-legacy primary at maximum one makes another handoff infeasible; release preflight refuses with runtime-release-capacity-raise-cap-required before any image mutation and requires an explicit cap increase. The image intent retains the exact permission checked by preflight, so later configuration changes fail the existing digest fence before publication. It never raises capacity or drains serving compute automatically. A settled public probe that expires or predates the current canonical policy needs a fresh probe and promotion attempt. The release journals the original startup manifest and each probe and promotion submission before sending. A fresh promotion may join current canonical policy to that exact startup witness only while the active boot and selection epoch still match. A started or unknown attempt permits inspection only. The next release uses the other application. A release still verifies the complete live provider baseline and image metadata before activation. Alchemy's reference-source hash stays separate from runtime provenance in the retained manifest. Changing the legacy image, configuration, namespace, placement or resource policy refuses preservation; a retained deletion policy alone is never a freeze. Fresh stages continue to build their own initial legacy image. Readiness and its dependent database budget verification are deferred together during intermediate phases, then run with their actual output and readiness receipt dependency after promotion and old-generation drain. The outer release completes only after those checks pass, selected runtime readiness proves the desired image and startup settings, and the host release report exposes image readiness. A matching Worker revision alone cannot advance bundle reconciliation."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Fleet launch permission is a signed, revision-checked deployment command naming both private application bindings, their provider configuration and the retained legacy boot. Provider refusal prevents activation. The resource reservation checks the current permission in the same transaction as its four-resource guard. Disabling permission stops growth and drains existing resources; unknown outcomes remain inspectable and never dispatch another launch. Planned replacement reserves its new resource before starting and retains the old ready runtime until the replacement is positively ready. The root serializes reservations with its durable fence, then waits for already reserved calls to reach their send boundary before dispatching endpoint drain through the same retained DO stub. Cancelled calls release that barrier without replay. A failed transport may retry only a model-list read or a request whose model send boundary was never crossed. An accepted model call is never replayed by the pool; native client retries are separate requests. A broken cached endpoint stub is replaced for future sends. Only a private read-only POST health exchange without the runtime-identity header before model dispatch may reconnect once after any thrown Error, excluding explicit overload, within its original signal and deadline. Runtime reconstruction may invalidate a retained stub without setting disconnected or retryable flags; missing flags therefore do not prevent this read-only reconnect. The successful health read, following model send and drain share the replacement stub. One controller endpoint failure record retains the first and optional second send, cell-local stub acquisition numbers, exact allowlisted exception names and codes, closed message classes and tri-state flags. It records method and private action classes, identity-header presence, reconnect eligibility and the repeat outcome without exception text, request bodies or header values. Its generated request identifier joins a terminal exception or refused response to the gateway refusal. Each settled send records its bounded elapsed milliseconds, and a refused response records its closed refusal code with a closed cause when one applies: startup deadline, control failure, or the storage, membership or signing dependency. The cause travels in its own header and survives one retained hop, so callers on an older release still parse the unchanged step and reason. Unknown names and codes remain explicitly other; missing flags remain distinct from false. HTTP refusals, non-Error throws, explicit overload, model requests and control mutations never gain another send. The endpoint serializes model admission with drain before non-storage health waits, so neither delayed headers nor active streams hold up the drain acknowledgment. Both durable counts must reach zero before retirement. Each admitted endpoint stream has an idempotent durable lease. Completion records a released marker before decrementing its count; reconstruction compacts only completed leases, preserving live and legacy unknown activity. A rejected decrement cannot memoize failure forever, and a lost acknowledgement after commit cannot decrement another stream. Retirement attempts emit only closed phase timings, receipt booleans and provider-absence results. Private endpoint response-header waits respect caller deadlines and cancel late responses without resending. Retirement acknowledgement and report-body waits share that caller bound. The endpoint attaches one platform stop monitor after its durable retiring intent and before destruction, then bounds each report wait to the same two seconds. A resolved monitor or a native Error carrying a nonzero integer exit status must also leave the platform running flag false. A nonzero status proves termination, never health. Pending observations, malformed exit status and unclassified monitor rejection cannot prove a stop or trigger another destruction. Reports after reconstruction may reattach observation without redispatching destruction. A root retiring intent resumes a before-dispatch retirement only after the child proves no retirement intent, a fresh known-zero drain and fresh provider presence. A retained child intent permits observation only. Primary retirement uses the same native stop and exact provider-absence requirements. A stopped platform observation alone still cannot replace exact fresh provider absence. A retained exact-boot retiring intent with known zero activity plus stopped local compute may recover a lost destroy reply by persisting its retirement receipt; unexpected stopped compute without that intent cannot use the ordinary retirement recovery. A quarantined exact boot has a separate failed-compute receipt after stopped local compute is positively observed. Its interrupted activity remains retained instead of being declared complete. Neither path repeats destruction. Capacity remains reserved until the separate current provider inventory positively proves absence. A missing endpoint drain acknowledgment keeps maintenance due even while the root still holds active streams. Ordinary authorized model traffic balances execution across the primary and fleet; management and shared account admission retain their original authority. Private peer interception is installed only after the exact launch witness is retained, and peer requests cannot start or warm compute. The controller resolves source and target through its owned live rows, while endpoint checks bind the actual launch or retained legacy boot. Opaque peer bytes retain Home mutual TLS. Both upgrade-bearing hops use private stub fetch, because custom RPC cannot serialize a WebSocket response. Read-only runtime fetches emit closed start, pending, cancellation and completion timings for stored configuration, identity, readiness headers and body, policy assertion and the actual management-port headers. These diagnostics never renew a lease, warm a runtime or print request data, credentials, identity or exception text. Runtime health headers and body share the maximum existing policy-lease deadline, raced explicitly against caller cancellation even when a container port ignores its signal. A timeout refuses health, cancels an owned body reader and cancels any late header response; it never grants stale authority, retries or replays work. Routing refusals retain a closed step and reason across fleet acquisition, selected identity, pre-send health and protection, and port lookup or dispatch. Private endpoint refusals carry only validated closed metadata across the binding; public failures and logs include that metadata without exception messages, provider bodies or credentials. Existing admission, exception identity and unknown-outcome replay rules remain unchanged. RPC carries only peer-source and live-authority observations. Container-bound loopback props overwrite incoming source headers; the public Worker rejects the private peer transport path, and Root and endpoint recheck retained source, launch and boot authority before opening a socket. Fleet deadlines share the controller alarm without advancing Home collection. Home maintenance runs as one retained waitUntil task per controller incarnation, allowing later native fleet alarms to fire while a Home dependency is held. Repeated Home deadlines join that task without duplicating maintenance and retain a five-second wake-up after consuming a due deadline. A failed Home task rearms at the existing five-second membership cadence instead of losing the consumed deadline. Alarm, deadline, Home and per-cell fleet phases emit closed timestamped events, including actual drain activity, without identities or exception text. Primary lifecycle warmth records its Home deadline through the same scheduler, so an earlier fleet alarm cannot hide that deadline or consume later upkeep. Reconstruction preserves both deadlines and held primary request counts."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "A runtime transport failure or refused health read commits an exact-execution quarantine before releasing its failed request reservation. Pending or rejected quarantine writes keep that reservation unresolved, including later client cancellation. A private native socket lifetime failure closes the data connection and commits quarantine before releasing its controller reservation. The runtime endpoint relays only the private ready and complete messages and counts that control lifetime once; model frames stay natively coupled. Endpoint data dispatch requires the matching ready control lifetime and exact execution, and the same control identifier cannot dispatch twice. Unsent calls check the local fence again at dispatch, and reconstruction reads the durable fence. Quarantined rows cannot become ready through ordinary observation, cannot supply new execution or be selected as a new peer destination, and remain inside the physical resource limit. An existing planned replacement covers the failed runtime; otherwise one durable replacement demand is retained and consumed by an authorized replacement reservation. New resources still require current fleet permission and its existing shared resource bound. Compute capacity is reclaimed only after the endpoint proves the exact process stopped and fresh provider inventory proves its absence. Failed launch tombstones preserve interrupted counts and accept late stream releases without claiming a terminal or replaying inference. A live transport casualty drains through the ordinary two-count zero-activity retirement path. Other runtimes and already dispatched streams retain their own lifetime and revocation authority. A quarantined source may finish its admitted peer work only while its exact live launch remains valid; it cannot receive new peer work. Each runtime endpoint class owns the native alarm used by its launch lifecycle. Alarm upkeep reads the retained launch target and encrypted configuration, validates the same boot and fresh health lease, renews only running admitted compute and rearms its own deadline. Endpoint alarm delivery cannot start missing, stopped or retired compute. The independent controller fleet alarm probes admitted selection and ready siblings every two seconds. Failed siblings enter the existing quarantine, absence proof and replacement path without requiring a discovering request. Selected stopped compute uses a fresh signed restart grant bound to the previous boot, unchanged sealed startup settings, current selection and fresh provider observations. The endpoint serializes admission and uses the durable launch ledger; lost replies join or observe that exact start. A positively stopped unacknowledged restart can consume a fresh grant and the checked ledger generation, while a running uncertain process is never destroyed or restarted. Restart readiness and Home work run as separate retained tasks so a cold start cannot consume the next fleet or membership deadline. Selection and the fleet primary identity change together after trusted launch and health proof. Expired positive policies grant nothing on a fresh boot; a retained completion record requires Home renewal, current native policy proof and peer bootstrap before recovery reports ready, including after controller reconstruction. Fenced, retired, changed-selection and changed-provider targets cannot be resurrected. The shared recovery deadline is two hundred seconds after detection, plus at most two seconds to the next due alarm and a two-second health probe when platform delivery and provider readiness meet those budgets. Healthy sibling routing continues independently of that wait. Recent model demand retains one sibling through the last demand plus sixty seconds, using the existing maximum and idle retirement thereafter; traffic-driven scale-out above that minimum is unchanged. Primary staging may share an application only with siblings on the exact same immutable generation, and still reserves primary headroom inside the existing physical bound. The demand timestamp survives reconstruction and idle alarms never renew it. Safe retry shares one bounded recovery deadline and caller cancellation, releases failed reservations and reacquires a healthy identity. Accepted healthy model calls retain their caller deadline while waiting for headers. Every legacy dispatch carries the same no-replay witness. Every successful supervisor pass clears only its observed primary failure, and stopped completion boots re-enter signed recovery. Health fencing precedes selection of the surviving warm minimum. Expired admission joins recovery before fresh authority is checked, and revocation still aborts that admission. An accepted preheader interruption returns provider-native retry advice; an interrupted Messages or Responses stream ends in its native transient error. Committed HTTP status cannot change. Other stream protocols retain their native transport interruption and require explicit client support rather than a fabricated status. No standing queue, admission cap or hedged model mutation is introduced."
    }
    metadata {
      constraint "Runtime admission joins release-owned provider observations to a private trusted-start witness. A live cell start owns activation until its reply and activation settle; concurrent upkeep leaves that exact target warming rather than racing a second activation. Duplicate calls share the owned start. Failure or reconstruction releases local ownership, so retained unowned starts still recover through observation without another compute start. A desired image binding or expected configuration hash is not evidence of the running image or its startup settings. A cold start acknowledgement never supplies the routing lease: after its single start send, the controller reads fresh health for that exact acknowledged boot. Only that health request consumes the lease window; delayed health, cancellation, identity drift and unknown start acknowledgements cannot authorize use or another start. The actual start callback retains the exact launch challenge and applied startup fingerprint before dispatch; authenticated readiness completes its boot. A failed witness write cannot start compute, and uncertain launch outcomes permit observation without a second start. Existing running compute may be preserved explicitly as unattested-active, never decoded as proven candidate evidence. Its adoption ACK and later passive readback derive the exact retained operation, manifest, decrypted capsule fingerprint, boot and committed selection from existing records. A fresh authenticated boot read and a selection recheck fence drift; capsule-only admission, another epoch, a candidate or draining slot, and an unknown ACK cannot authorize first-handoff advancement. Only a freshly joined attested candidate may pass promotion, with current controller, active selection and full positive and retired policy revisions still checked. The shared pool-runtime-attestation-v1 codec leaves existing slot and launch meanings unchanged. Private settings remain encrypted and stay out of receipts and logs. A manual main-only deployment credential audit reads token verification and self metadata without installing dependencies or making a provider write. Its output contains only status and permission-group names; unknown metadata does not establish token-creation permission. Ciphertext-corruption fixtures must change the decoded bytes and verify that precondition before expecting witness refusal; changing hex case alone is not corruption. Normal requests and explicit upkeep renew the selected runtime idle timer; synthetic probes and private health or witness reads never do. The legacy recovery command cannot restart an admitted runtime; only its independently supervised signed restart path can do so. Initial adoption waits for any already admitted legacy restart to finish. Provider observations identify the exact named instance within a multi-instance application. Every active sibling must be running the same admitted image; an absent target, changed application settings or active rollout refuses admission. Instance-list ordering does not change observation identity. A runtime class stays unbound until its idle application provisioning is acknowledged and read back; a binding alone cannot admit compute. A separate fleet ledger reserves before launch, counts warming and uncertain resources against the four-runtime guard, and retains held execution counts across reconstruction. Lost acknowledgements cannot dispatch a second launch or release capacity twice. Drain rejects delayed readiness; retired launch tombstones remain outside the bounded active-resource list. Logical cells isolate handoff journals while reading the existing canonical membership record, and cannot write that authority or start the legacy container. Scheduling state alone neither attests a runtime nor proves its endpoint has drained."
    }
  }
}
